  "strategy": "### 1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe approach employs a cutting-plane method inspired by semi-infinite optimization. It iteratively tightens constraints based on the most restrictive shifts, aiming to minimize the maximum overlap without discretizing h. This method balances exploration of critical shifts with efficient constraint refinement, leveraging convex optimization for local improvements.\n\n### 2. Mathematical specification\n\n- Let h \u2208 [0,1]^{n_points} satisfy \u2211h = n_points / 2.\n- Define overlap(k) := \u2211h[x](1 - h[x + k]) * dx for each shift k.\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k overlap(k), where shifts k are in [0, 2] with wrap-around disallowed.\n- Each constraint for shift k enforces overlap(k) \u2264 C\u2085.\n\n### 3. Implementation procedure\n\n#### 1. Initialization\n- Use initial_h_values as starting h.\n- Initialize C\u2085 as the maximum overlap of h.\n\n#### 2. Cutting-plane loop\nFor up to 200 iterations or budget exhaustion:\n1. For current h:\n   a. Compute overlap for shifts k in a fine grid (e.g., n_points * 2).\n   b. Identify the shift k_max where overlap(k_max) is maximum.\n2. Add a constraint: overlap(k_max \u00b1 \u0394k) \u2264 C\u2085, using a small \u0394k to capture peak.\n3. Formulate a QP to minimize C\u2085 with new constraints.\n4. Solve QP using CVXOPT or similar.\n5. Update h and C\u2085 if improvement found.\n\n#### 3. Termination\n- Stop when budget exhausted or C\u2085 improvement < 1e-5.\n\n### 4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Initial shift grid density: 2 * n_points.\n- k_max neighborhood width: \u0394k = 1/n_points.\n- Constraint addition per iteration: 3-5 around k_max.\n\n### 5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Each QP solve: ~1e3 - 1e4 operations.\n- Total operations: ~2e5, manageable within 1100s.\n\n### 6. Validation and return contract\n- Verify h \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h) exact.\n- Recompute C\u2085 with evaluator.",
